# /no-human-review — Autonomous Mode Flag

Add `/no-human-review` to one of these commands:

- `/orches-sdlc <feature description> /no-human-review`
- `/orches-sdlc-jira <PROJ-123 or URL> /no-human-review`

The flag can appear anywhere in the arguments. Match it case-insensitively and ignore it when reading the feature description or the Jira key. Open every reply with `🧭 orchestrator`, and show the status board (agent, stage, state) at each step.

Invoke `using-agent-skills` to confirm the workflow.

## What changes, stage by stage

| Stage | With the flag |
|---|---|
| 0. Jira card (`/orches-sdlc-jira` only) | Unchanged. Stops if the Atlassian MCP server is not connected |
| 1. Spec | Still needs your approval |
| 2. Plan and plan review | Still needs your agreement. Revisions repeat until you agree |
| 3. Build | Unchanged. Unit tests first, one commit per task, frontend and backend tasks run in parallel when both are ready |
| 4. Build review | Unchanged. Each task is reviewed by another agent of the same role, up to 3 rounds |
| 5. Build acceptance | Automatic once every build review approves. The build is not presented for approval |
| 6. PR description | Written as usual |
| 7. PR decision | Automatic. If a git host is connected, `devops-engineer` creates the branch, commits the remaining changes, pushes, and opens the PR without asking. If no git host is connected, the title and description are shown and the run stops |

## Still stops for you

- an ambiguous spec, or a Jira card with missing information;
- a missing Atlassian connection, when using `/orches-sdlc-jira`;
- a failing test that cannot be fixed, after `debugging-and-error-recovery` has been tried;
- a review still open after 3 rounds;
- any irreversible step outside creating the PR.

## Never, even with the flag

- merging a pull request;
- releasing or deploying to production;
- pushing to the default branch;
- committing files outside the current feature, or using `git add -A`.

## If run on its own

If `/no-human-review` is run without a feature description or a Jira card, ask the user which one to run. For a Jira card, ask for the key or URL and check the Atlassian connection first. Then run the matching flow with the flag applied, following the stages of `/orches-sdlc` or `/orches-sdlc-jira`.
